course: everyday
lesson: 406
title: Toxic players, swearing and «fishcakes»
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kai was having a perfectly nice Fortnite evening until a stranger on voice chat started calling him an idiot. Not once — over and over, like a broken doorbell. Kai went hot all over and his fingers were already typing something worse back. Then he remembered his dad's four words: mute, block, report, tell. He muted the man (the silence was wonderful), blocked him, and his dad showed him the report button. The stranger was still shouting somewhere, but now he was shouting at nobody.
explain: Players who are nasty on purpose are called toxic, and toxic is catching. Leo got called «trash» by a teenager one evening, and ten minutes later he typed «trash» at a little kid on the other team. The kid left the game straight away. Leo stared at the empty space where the kid had been and felt awful — he had just handed the meanness on, like a hot potato. Theo nearly did the same: he typed a long nasty message to a player who beat him, read it back, deleted every word and sent «gg» instead.
explain: Swear words travel too. Ollie's friend taught him a word he had heard in a game, and Ollie, feeling very grown-up, tried it out at Sunday dinner. His grandma dropped her fork, the gravy went everywhere and Ollie lost his pudding. A word that sounds cool in a headset sounds very different over roast potatoes.
explain: Noah had a better idea. When he loses, he shouts made-up words: «FISHCAKES!» and «BUBBLEBUM!» The whole squad copied him, then his mum started saying «fishcakes» when she burned the toast. Anger has to go somewhere, and a silly word carries it away without hurting anyone. And when Zara saw a player write racist things in chat, she reported him, though she was nervous it would not matter. Two days later a message came: the player had been banned.
explain: The others found their own ways. When her team called Priya «noob» for losing a fight, she said «I'm learning» and, when they kept on, left the team. Jayden's squad got a player who screamed every time they lost, so they left the lobby and played with their real friends instead. Ruby heard grown-up strangers swearing on voice chat and muted all voice chat in the settings. And when Mia's mum overheard Mia's friends swearing, Mia asked them to keep it clean in her games, and they did.
fact: A toxic player is someone who is nasty to other players on purpose.
fact: Mute, block, report and tell a grown-up are the four tools against a nasty player.
fact: Muting someone means you stop hearing them; it does not tell them anything.
fact: Reports are read by the game company, and players can be banned because of them.
fact: Copying a mean player spreads the meanness to someone new.
fact: Reading a message again before sending gives you a last chance to change your mind.
fact: Words picked up from games are often not words you can use at home or at school.
fact: Grown-up strangers on voice chat may swear, and you can turn voice chat off or set it to friends only.
fact: Insults say more about the person typing them than about the person they are aimed at.
fact: A silly made-up word can let out anger without hurting anyone.
fact: You can ask your friends to keep the language clean when you are playing together.
exercise: What did Kai do when the stranger kept calling him an idiot? | He muted, blocked and reported him and told his dad | He shouted worse words straight back down the mic at him | He kept listening so he would not look scared | He gave the stranger his own name to complain
exercise: How did Kai feel when the insults started? | Hot all over and wanting to shout back worse | Calm and relaxed, because it was only a silly game | Glad that someone online had finally noticed him | Bored and sleepy, because it was nearly bedtime
exercise: What does muting a player do? | You stop hearing him, and he is not told | It deletes his account for ever, with all his games | It sends him a warning message | It makes him hear you better
exercise: Why did Leo feel awful after calling a little kid «trash»? | He saw the kid leave and knew he had passed the meanness on | His mum heard it and took his console away for the whole week | The kid called him a worse name back | He lost the match because of it
exercise: What does «toxic» mean about a player? | He is nasty to other players on purpose | He is very good at the game and wins every match | He plays for too many hours every single night | He plays on a slow old computer with lots of lag
exercise: What did Theo do with his long nasty message? | Read it back, deleted it and typed «gg» | Sent it to the whole server so everyone could read it | Saved it to send the next day when nobody was looking | Asked Leo to send it for him from his account
exercise: What happened when Ollie used the game swear word at dinner? | Grandma dropped her fork and he lost his pudding | Everyone laughed and gave him a second helping of pudding | Nobody noticed at all | His grandma taught him a worse one
exercise: Why were Noah's «fishcakes» and «bubblebum» a wise idea? | They let out his anger without hurting anyone | They are secret codes for cheating that the game can't see | They make the other team lose | They are the real names of game moves
exercise: How did Zara feel when she reported the racist player? | Nervous that it would not matter at all | Sure he would be banned within a minute | Scared that she would be the one banned herself | Happy to get him into trouble
exercise: What happened after Zara's report? | Two days later the player was banned | Nothing, because reports are never read by anyone | Zara got banned instead | The player sent her a thank-you note
exercise: Priya's team calls her «noob» for losing a fight. What is a wise answer? | «I'm learning», or just leave the team | Call them worse names until they stop | Quit games for ever | Pretend she lost on purpose to help the team
exercise: Jayden's squad gets a player who screams every time they lose. What did they do? | Left the lobby and played with real friends | Screamed back louder | Stayed and let him scream all evening long | Gave him their passwords to calm him
exercise: Ruby muted all voice chat after hearing grown-ups swear. Was that sensible? | Yes, settings are there to protect her | No, she must listen to every player in the game | No, muting is a kind of cheating | Only if her friends muted too
exercise: Mia's mum overhears Mia's friends swearing in voice chat. What can Mia do? | Ask them to keep it clean in her games | Swear even more so she fits in with them | Stop being friends with them for ever | Tell them her mum likes it
exercise: Which is a kind of courage that is not shouting back? | Muting the bully and telling an adult | Typing the nastiest insult you know | Following him into the next game to get even | Daring him to meet you in real life
exercise: Why do insults say more about the person typing them? | They show his own bad mood, not your worth | They are always true about you, even if they hurt | They prove he is the best player | They come from the game, not the person
test: «this guy keeps calling me thick on voice, wot do i do» | Mute him, block, report, tell an adult | Call him thicker back, he started it | Keep listening, he'll get bored | Give him ur address to sort it out
test: mate: «say the word, go on, everyone says it in game». At gran's table? | Don't, it's not a word for home | Say it, gran won't know what it means anyway | Say it twice so it sounds normal | Whisper it to the dog under the table
test: «he beat me, typing him a proper essay of insults rn» — wise move? | Read it back, delete it, send gg | Send it, he deserves every word of it | Send it to his mates too so they know | Add a threat at the end to scare him
test: «that random called me noob, i'm quitting the game forever». What's true? | One insult says more about him than about you | He's right, so you should quit and find another game | Noobs are banned from games | Only noobs get called noob
test: «this squad bloke screams every time we die, it's doing my head in». Best plan? | Leave the lobby, play with your real mates | Scream louder so he stops screaming first | Stay, it's rude to leave | Mute your own mic
test: Leo: «a teen called me trash, so I called a little kid trash. that's fair innit?» | No, he just passed the meanness on | Yes, fair is fair, that's how games work | Yes, the little kid started it | Only if the kid was on the other team
test: Zara: «reporting is pointless, nobody reads them». Right? | Wrong, reports get read and players get banned | Right, reports go nowhere | Right, only adults can report | Wrong, reports go to the police only, never to the game
test: Noah lost again: «FISHCAKES!» What is he doing? | Letting anger out with a harmless silly word | Swearing at his teammates in a secret way | Ordering dinner on voice chat | Giving his team a secret signal to retreat
test: teacher: «Ollie, where did you even hear that word?» Honest answer? | «In a game, miss, I didn't know it was that bad» | «I made it up just now» | «My grandma taught me it when I was little» | «It's not a real word»
test: «grown-ups swear on voice all the time, can't stop em». What can you do? | Turn voice chat off or set it to friends only | Nothing, you just have to sit and hear it | Swear back at them until they stop | Unplug the router
test: Mia to her squad: «oi, keep it clean, my mum's in the kitchen». Is that ok? | Yes, you can set the tone in your own games | No, friends must say whatever they want to | No, only adults can make rules | Yes, but only while mum listens
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with established guidance where a pupil is subjected to repeated verbal abuse by an unknown participant in an online game. | The participant ought to be muted, blocked and reported, and an adult informed | The pupil ought to reply in terms of equal or greater severity | The pupil ought to continue listening so as not to appear afraid of the participant | The pupil ought to disclose his identity so that the matter may be settled
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of a pupil who, having been insulted, subsequently insults a younger participant? | The said conduct transmits the hostility to a further person | The said conduct discharges the hostility entirely and harms nobody | The said conduct is justified where the first insult was severe | The said conduct is without effect upon the younger participant
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning reports submitted in respect of offensive conduct in online games. | Reports are reviewed by the operator and may result in exclusion of the offender | Reports are disregarded in all cases | Reports result in the exclusion of the reporting pupil in place of the offender | Reports are forwarded exclusively to the school concerned
exam: Ascertain the principal merit of a pupil substituting an invented and inoffensive exclamation for profane language upon defeat. | Frustration is expressed without injury to any other person | Defeat is thereby converted into victory | The opponent is thereby compelled to withdraw from the game at once | The pupil is thereby exempted from household rules
